12_23_23_28_03	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|NBF0|RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': 'Okay, let\'s see. I\'m playing as White in this chess game. The current board state is the standard starting position. All the white pieces are on their initial squares: pawns on the second rank, and the back rank has rooks, knights, bishops, queen, and king. The black pieces are mirrored on the eighth and seventh ranks.\n\nMy valid moves are all the possible first moves for White. Since it\'s the start of the game, the pawns can move either one or two squares forward, and the knights can make their initial L-shaped moves. The valid moves listed are:\n\n- Pawns: a2-a4, a2-a3, b2-b4, b2-b3, c2-c4, c2-c3, d2-d4, d2-d3, e2-e4, e2-e3, f2-f4, f2-f3, g2-g4, g2-g3, h2-h4, h2-h3\n- Knights: b1-a3, b1-c3, g1-f3, g1-h3\n\nSo, the first move options are typical opening moves. Now, I need to choose the best move to maximize my chances of winning.\n\nIn chess openings, the main principles are controlling the center, developing pieces, and ensuring king safety. The most common strong first moves for White are e2-e4 (king\'s pawn) and d2-d4 (queen\'s pawn), as they immediately contest the center. e4 is particularly popular because it opens lines for the queen and bishop.\n\nLooking at the valid moves, e2-e4 is available. Let\'s check if it\'s in the list. Yes, "P: e2-e4" is one of the options. Similarly, d2-d4 is also there. Other pawn moves like c2-c4 (English opening) or f2-f4 (Bird\'s opening) are less common but still playable. The knight moves like Nf3 or Nc3 are also standard, developing a piece and preparing to control the center.\n\nBut since it\'s the very first move, moving a pawn to the center is usually better than moving a knight immediately, except maybe for Nf3 which is a common alternative. However, in the list, the knight moves from g1 are to f3 or h3. Nf3 is a strong move, but e4 is even more central.\n\nWait, the valid moves include N: g1-f3. That\'s a standard move. But e4 is often considered the best first move because it stakes a strong claim in the center.\n\nLet me think about the pros and cons. e4 controls d5 and f5 squares, opens the diagonal for the bishop and queen. It\'s aggressive and leads to open games. d4 is similar but maybe a bit more solid. However, e4 is the most popular and statistically one of the best.\n\nIn the given valid moves, both e2-e4 and d2-d4 are present. But why would someone choose one over the other? Well, e4 is slightly more central and allows for faster development of the king\'s bishop.\n\nAlso, considering that the opponent is Black, who will likely respond by challenging the center. But as White, getting the center first is key.\n\nAnother thing: moving a pawn two squares (like e2-e4) is standard, and it\'s better than moving one square (e2-e3) because it gains more space.\n\nLooking at the list, the options for e-pawn are e2-e4 and e2-e3. e2-e4 is better here.\n\nSimilarly, d2-d4 is good, but e4 is often preferred.\n\nThe knight moves: Nf3 is good, but on the first move, it\'s slightly less common than e4 or d4. However, it\'s still a strong move. But since the question is to maximize winning chances, e4 is probably the best.\n\nWait, but in some contexts, like if you want to avoid certain lines, but here it\'s the starting position, so e4 is the way to go.\n\nAlso, moving the e-pawn two squares allows the bishop to develop later. For example, after e4, next moves could be Nf3, Bc4, etc.\n\nSo, the best move here is likely e2-e4.\n\nLet me check if there\'s any reason not to play e4. In the starting position, all pieces are in place, so no issues with pins or anything. The pawn is protected by the queen and king\'s knight, but since it\'s the first move, there\'s no immediate threat.\n\nYes, e2-e4 is the strongest first move here.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be P: e2-e4.', 'output': "Situation: The game is at the standard starting position with all pieces on their initial squares. As White, I have the first move advantage. The board is symmetric, and both sides have all pieces unmoved, with pawns on the second (White) and seventh (Black) ranks. Key strategic goals are controlling the center, developing pieces efficiently, and preparing for king safety.\n\nEvaluation: \n- **e2-e4 (King's Pawn Opening)**: Immediately seizes central control (d5/f5 squares), opens diagonals for the queen and light-squared bishop, and aligns with classical opening principles. Statistically, this is the most successful first move for White in practice.\n- **d2-d4 (Queen's Pawn Opening)**: Also strong for central control but slightly less aggressive; often leads to closed/positional games. Less dynamic than e4 for immediate initiative.\n- **N: g1-f3**: Develops a knight and prepares kingside castling, but delays central pawn play. Better after initial pawn moves (e.g., after e4).\n- **Other pawn moves (e.g., c2-c4, f2-f4)**: Less central impact; c4 (English) is solid but slower, while f4 (Bird’s) weakens kingside safety prematurely.\n\nReason: e2-e4 maximizes central influence, accelerates development, and creates immediate tactical opportunities. It’s the highest-probability winning move according to opening theory and historical data, as it directly challenges Black to respond under pressure while optimizing White’s initiative.\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4"}
