nitiative; Black can still challenge later (e.g., e6-e5) without immediate compensation.\n- **B: f4-e5**: Proactive but allows Black to capture d5 first (exd5), reducing White's advantage.\n- **P: b2-b4**: Weakens queenside pawn structure after axb3, with minimal strategic gain.\n\nReason: **P: d5-e6** maximizes winning chances by forcing Black into a vulnerable position. After dxe6 Kxe6, **Be5+** forks the king and disrupts Black's coordination, leveraging the exposed king for sustained pressure. This dynamic play outweighs quieter alternatives.\n\nAnswer: P: d5-e6"}
